2025-05-20 23:27:02,"{""skill"": ""Function of Sentence"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""Film historian Eugenia Farnsworth contends that early musicals often relegated women to secondary or decorative roles, overshadowed by charismatic male leads. Yet, she has discovered that several productions from the 1930s subtly challenged these conventions, portraying women as witty figures whose calculated maneuvers alter the course of the narrative. According to Farnsworth, many films deliberately gave female characters comedic solos or well-timed interjections, suggesting an incipient resistance to the industry’s entrenched hierarchy. _Though overshadowed by the patriarchal structures of filmmaking, these female characters intermittently assume narrative control that subverts the era’s expectations._ Farnsworth’s analysis indicates that classic Hollywood’s representation of women was more multifaceted than is often assumed.\n\nWhich choice best describes the function of the underlined sentence in the passage as a whole?"", ""choices"": [""A. It presents background details on Farnsworth’s research that complicate her argument about overlooked female comedic solos."", ""B. It clarifies that even though the creators’ patriarchal bias remained influential, female roles periodically defied those constraints and gained narrative weight."", ""C. It claims that earlier musicals from the 1930s systematically eliminated patriarchal influence by positioning female characters in leadership roles."", ""D. It questions whether comedic interjections by female characters can undermine traditional social norms regarding gender roles.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""Choice B is the best answer because the underlined sentence emphasizes how, despite dominant patriarchal structures, the films depict women stepping into positions of influence that temporarily upend traditional gender norms. By specifying that women “intermittently assume narrative control,” Farnsworth reveals a nuanced dynamic: the films do not fully escape patriarchal influences but do provide moments of female agency. In contrast, choice A misreads the underlined sentence as background details rather than a statement of thematic balance. Choice C incorrectly suggests that the patriarchal influence was eradicated, which the underlined sentence does not claim. Choice D misses the passage’s main idea, focusing on whether comedic moments alone question social norms rather than pointing to the subtle but tangible ways these roles actually subvert the expectations of the time period."", ""topic"": ""The portrayal of gender roles in classic Hollywood films""}",Function of Sentence,Hard,"Film historian Eugenia Farnsworth contends that early musicals often relegated women to secondary or decorative roles, overshadowed by charismatic male leads. Yet, she has discovered that several productions from the 1930s subtly challenged these conventions, portraying women as witty figures whose calculated maneuvers alter the course of the narrative. According to Farnsworth, many films deliberately gave female characters comedic solos or well-timed interjections, suggesting an incipient resistance to the industry’s entrenched hierarchy. _Though overshadowed by the patriarchal structures of filmmaking, these female characters intermittently assume narrative control that subverts the era’s expectations._ Farnsworth’s analysis indicates that classic Hollywood’s representation of women was more multifaceted than is often assumed.

