ger if it referenced specific phrases in the text to bolster the reasoning behind rejecting the other options."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""The question is appropriately labeled as Easy. It requires a single-step inference that directly connects a phrase in the passage with the conclusion about traditional media companies rethinking their strategies. To further solidify its Easy status, minor adjustments to the distractors to avoid any ambiguity would be beneficial, but overall the question meets the criteria for an easy-difficulty SAT inference question.""}"
2025-05-20 23:30:55,"{""skill"": ""Inferences"", ""difficulty"": ""Easy"", ""question"": ""In Shakespeare’s The Tempest, Prospero, the exiled Duke of Milan, seizes control of a remote island and compels its original inhabitant, Caliban, to serve him and obey his commands. Though Caliban despises Prospero’s strict rule and harsh threats, he has little choice but to comply because of Prospero’s considerable magical powers.\n\nWhich statement about Caliban’s perspective is best supported by the passage?"", ""choices"": [""A. He considers Prospero a generous figure who deserves unwavering loyalty."", ""B. He views performing his duties for Prospero as a way to strengthen their bond."", ""C. He resents Prospero’s control and longs for the freedom he once possessed on the island."", ""D. He believes Prospero’s strong authority grants him a more secure life on the island.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""Choice C is correct because Caliban’s resentment of Prospero’s commanding nature, along with his desire to recover the independence he lost, can be inferred from the fact that he despises serving Prospero’s orders yet must obey. Choices A, B, and D are inconsistent with the information that Caliban resents his forced servitude, so these other options are not supported by the passage’s description of their relationship."", ""topic"": ""The power dynamics in Prospero’s rule over Caliban in The Tempest""}",Inferences,Easy,"In Shakespeare’s The Tempest, Prospero, the exiled Duke of Milan, seizes control of a remote island and compels its original inhabitant, Caliban, to serve him and obey his commands. Though Caliban despises Prospero’s strict rule and harsh threats, he has little choice but to comply because of Prospero’s considerable magical powers.

