2025-05-20 23:33:00,"{""skill"": ""Inferences"", ""difficulty"": ""Easy"", ""question"": ""Researchers examined a group of people who read online news articles that reinforced their existing political views. The participants shared these articles widely on social media and rarely explored alternative viewpoints, suggesting they favored content that agreed with their initial opinions.\n\nWhich inference is best supported by the information in the text?"", ""choices"": [""A. People who read articles online are more likely to switch political views if they see a single opposing viewpoint."", ""B. People who share news articles are primarily concerned about verifying the accuracy of the content they post."", ""C. People are generally unaware that the articles they read have any impact on their political views."", ""D. People tend to select information that supports the viewpoints they already hold.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""The passage describes participants sharing articles that confirm their current political opinions and seldom considering alternative perspectives. From this behavior, we can infer that people tend to select information supporting their preexisting beliefs. Choices A, B, and C are not strongly supported because the text does not provide evidence that anyone changes views based on a single opposing viewpoint, that they are primarily concerned about content accuracy, or that they are generally unaware of an article’s influence."", ""topic"": ""The role of cognitive biases in shaping political opinions""}",Inferences,Easy,"Researchers examined a group of people who read online news articles that reinforced their existing political views. The participants shared these articles widely on social media and rarely explored alternative viewpoints, suggesting they favored content that agreed with their initial opinions.

Which inference is best supported by the information in the text?","A. People who read articles online are more likely to switch political views if they see a single opposing viewpoint.
