2025-05-20 23:38:48,"{""skill"": ""Inferences"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""Ludwig van Beethoven is often celebrated for radically expanding the expressive range of Classical music while still honoring many of the musical forms he inherited from predecessors such as Joseph Haydn and Wolfgang Amadeus Mozart. Early in his career, he gained recognition for highly dramatic and intense works, and these bold methods never fully disappeared from his later compositions. In his middle period, Beethoven grew increasingly experimental, transforming the symphony and sonata into grand canvases for emotional depth. By the time he wrote his Ninth Symphony, many listeners hailed him as having ushered in a new era of Romantic expression.\n\nWhich inference about Beethoven’s compositional approach is best supported by the passage?"", ""choices"": [""A. He retained Classical structures in his compositions even as he developed new forms of musical expression."", ""B. He wrote only within the Classical tradition, rejecting any stylistic experiments as too indulgent."", ""C. He composed his early works in a Romantic style similar to his late-career symphonies."", ""D. He was uninterested in maintaining any connections to older forms once he transitioned to Romantic ideals.""], ""answer"": ""A"", ""explanation"": ""Choice A is the best answer because it directly connects the discussion of Beethoven’s early reliance on Classical traditions to his later willingness to expand forms in dramatic ways. The passage states that he honored existing forms from composers like Haydn while experimenting with expressive techniques, which suggests that he balanced older structures with innovative ideas. Choices B, C, and D are not well supported by the text: B and D ignore Beethoven’s reputation for blending old and new elements, and C conflicts with the passage’s statement that his Romantic approach developed over time."", ""topic"": ""The musical genius of Ludwig van Beethoven""}",Inf