2025-05-20 23:42:46,"{""skill"": ""Inferences"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""During the American Civil War, the three-day Battle of Gettysburg (July 1‒3, 1863) became an inflection point with consequences that reverberated far beyond Pennsylvania. Although the Confederate Army had previously experienced successes in the eastern theater, its defeat at Gettysburg checked the momentum that some believed would facilitate a maneuver toward Washington, D.C. Furthermore, the Union’s resolve was invigorated by what many hailed as a defensive triumph of considerable magnitude, emboldening officials to intensify their strategic commitments. Some historians contend that this pivotal Union victory, combined with Confederate supply shortages, deterred any subsequent large-scale thrusts into Union territory. Others maintain that while Gettysburg was significant, structural disadvantages in Confederate resources were already eroding Southern prospects as the war progressed.\n\nWhich inference about the battle’s influence on the Civil War is best supported by the passage?"", ""choices"": [""A. Gettysburg prevented both Union and Confederate forces from seeking foreign alliances to bolster their armies."", ""B. A conclusive Confederate victory at Gettysburg would have ended the Civil War immediately by securing Washington, D.C."", ""C. Gettysburg’s outcome curtailed Confederate confidence in long-range invasions and intensified Union morale for the remainder of the conflict."", ""D. The loss at Gettysburg proved irrelevant to Confederate strategies, as their supply lines were already robust at that point.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""From the passage, readers learn that the Confederate defeat at Gettysburg halted the momentum necessary for deeper northern incursions, while simultaneously boosting Union determination. Although the Confederates had earlier achieved victories, the passage suggests that the defeat at Gettysburg diminished their willingness to 