ltural practices in Japan."", ""C. Western creators have largely avoided elements of anime and manga due to significant cultural differences."", ""D. The popularity of anime and manga is limited to a small minority of viewers within Western countries.""], ""answer"": ""A"", ""explanation"": ""Choice A is correct because the text highlights how anime and manga have introduced new storytelling strategies, appealing to Western audiences and artists and inspiring fresh creative approaches. By focusing on the enthusiasm for these Japanese art forms and their influence on filmmaking, television, and illustration, the passage’s main idea is that anime and manga helped shape Western media in significant ways.\nChoice B is incorrect because the text does not focus on historical cultural practices. Choice C is incorrect because the text suggests the opposite: Western creators often adopt methods from anime and manga. Choice D is incorrect because the text states anime and manga have achieved widespread popularity, not just limited appeal."", ""topic"": ""The cultural impact of anime and manga on Western storytelling and media"", ""total_score"": 28, ""constructive_feedback"": ""The question is clearly written, logically constructed, and aligns well with SAT main idea questions. The passage provides a straightforward main idea that is directly supported by answer A, while the distractors are clearly incorrect. The explanation effectively outlines why choice A is correct and why the other choices are not, though it could benefit from citing specific textual cues to enhance students’ understanding. For further improvement, consider ensuring a distinct line break between the passage and the question prompt, which would enhance clarity in presentation."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""The difficulty level is well calibrated for an easy question. The passage presents an explicit main idea that requires only a single-step deduction to arrive at the correct answer. This aligns perfectly with the guidelines for easy SAT questions. Slight refinements in formatting to emphasize the separation between the text and the question may further aid student comprehension without altering the intended difficulty.""}"
2025-05-20 23:52:54,"{""skill"": ""Main Idea"", ""difficulty"": ""Easy"", ""question"": ""The following text is adapted from William Golding’s 1954 novel Lord of the Flies. In this excerpt, Ralph, the elected leader of a group of boys stranded on an island, strives to keep them organized and focused on being rescued.\n\n“We’ve got to keep the smoke going,\"" Ralph insisted, lifting the conch. “Otherwise, how will anyone find us? People need to see smoke,” he said firmly. Despite reminding them daily about the shelters and the signal fire, Ralph noticed the others slipping away to play or hunt. He held the conch, but it seemed no one listened as they once had.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?"", ""choices"": [""A. The boys are consistently enthusiastic about working together."", ""B. Ralph struggles to maintain order as the other boys lose interest in rescue."", ""C. The boys immediately reject Ralph’s requests for help collecting food."", ""D. Ralph decides it is more important to gather fruit than keep the fire going.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""The passage focuses on Ralph’s attempts to keep the boys motivated and united, especially regarding the signal fire for rescue, but the others begin to dismiss his instructions, preferring to hunt or play. Choice B most accurately summarizes this central problem of Ralph losing influence as the others lose interest in maintaining order and the fire."", ""topic"": ""Ralph’s leadership struggles in Lord of the Flies""}",Main Idea,Easy,"The following text is adapted from William Golding’s 1954 novel Lord of the Flies. In this excerpt, Ralph, the elected leader of a group of boys stranded on an island, strives to keep them organized and focused on being rescued.

