2025-05-21 00:04:53,"{""skill"": ""Main Idea"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""The following text is adapted from an essay discussing Franz Kafka’s novel The Trial, first published in 1925. The excerpt highlights how its main character, Josef K., becomes entangled in a surreal legal process. Though Josef K. never learns what crime he allegedly committed, he feels compelled to defend himself before a constantly shifting court whose representatives offer vague instructions and contradictory information. The more Josef K. tries to understand the rules of his case, the more tangled he becomes in an impersonal, nameless system that threatens his sense of personal freedom.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?"", ""choices"": [""A. The passage contrasts Josef K.’s hesitance to defend himself with the success he achieves in his trial."", ""B. The passage focuses on how Josef K. relies on acquaintances and allies who provide clear guidance about his case."", ""C. The passage highlights Josef K.’s relief when the authorities finally explain the reasons for his arrest."", ""D. The passage illustrates Josef K.’s struggle against a mysterious bureaucracy that undermines his autonomy.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""Choice D is correct because the text emphasizes that Josef K. becomes ensnared in a bureaucratic system he cannot fully understand, which threatens his sense of personal freedom. Kafka’s portrayal centers on the existential anxiety Josef K. experiences when faced with the unclear nature of the charges and the opaque court procedures. The other choices are less accurate because the passage does not mention any success in Josef K.’s trial (A), any reliance on helpful acquaintances who clarify his situation (B), or any relief tied to receiving a clear explanation of his alleged crime (C)."", ""topic"": ""The existential themes in The Trial by Franz Kafka""}",Main Idea,Medium,"The following text is adapted from an essay discussing 