2025-05-21 00:06:56,"{""skill"": ""Main Idea"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""The following text is adapted from a mid-20th-century historian’s account of early 19th-century Europe:\n\nNapoleon’s conquests not only reshaped national boundaries but triggered far-reaching transformations in European governance. Although his empire dissolved swiftly after his defeat, the upheavals he unleashed weakened traditional aristocratic systems and cultivated new ambitions for constitutional government. Sovereigns were compelled to negotiate alliances with former adversaries, navigate restive citizenries calling for reforms, and grapple with overlapping claims to territory. As the restoration of old dynasties proceeded, reformers seized on Napoleon’s legacy of central administrative organization, which some leaders co-opted to consolidate their own power. Even the Congress of Vienna, intended to reinstate a conservative balance of power, carried lasting effects by implicitly recognizing that a nation’s identity could not be rigidly imposed. Ultimately, the era’s coalition-building and revolutionary stirrings would reverberate well beyond the Empire’s collapse, leading to the eventual rise of nationalism and modern statecraft.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?\n"", ""choices"": [""A. Napoleon’s style of organizational management typified the main cause of Europe’s continued instability in the nineteenth century."", ""B. Hostile relations among monarchs prompted Europe’s aristocratic families to restore traditional boundaries throughout the continent."", ""C. The Napoleonic Wars provoked enduring ideological and structural changes in Europe’s political order, influencing both monarchs and reformers long after Napoleon’s fall."", ""D. Despite far-reaching conquests, Napoleon’s empire had no permanent geopolitical impact and largely preserved prewar social hierarchies.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""Choice C is correct because the text explains that Napoleon’s campaigns weakened aristocratic systems and spurred new calls for constitutional government, reshaping Europe’s balance of power and facilitating the rise of nationalism. The passage indicates that the Congress of Vienna could not entirely undo those changes, showing that the conflicts had lasting repercussions well beyond Napoleon’s defeat.\n\nChoice A is incorrect because the text does not characterize Napoleon’s administrative approach as the main cause of ongoing unrest; instead, administrators and monarchs selectively adapted his system. Choice B is incorrect because it treats the restoration of traditional boundaries as a simple outcome of hostile monarchic relations, whereas the passage highlights deeper structural changes such as constitutional reforms and nationalism. Choice D is incorrect because the text contrasts the profound shifts in European governance with any notion of no permanent impact or unaltered social organization."", ""topic"": ""The effects of the Napoleonic Wars on European politics""}",Main Idea,Hard,"The following text is adapted from a mid-20th-century historian’s account of early 19th-century Europe:

