2025-05-21 00:13:45,"{""skill"": ""Main Idea"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""The following text is adapted from a 21st-century commentary on the enduring impact of William Shakespeare’s works. The author discusses how Shakespeare’s plays continue to shape narrative structures in modern film, television, and stage productions, emphasizing that the Bard’s explorations of universal themes—such as regal ambition, unrequited devotion, and comedic misunderstandings—remain highly relevant to contemporary audiences. Additionally, the commentary posits that Shakespeare revolutionized dramatic storytelling by integrating rich layers of characterization, cunning wordplay, and metamorphic shifts in tone, setting a precedent for future generations of playwrights, screenwriters, and novelists. Although some critics argue that referencing Shakespeare has become merely a cultural shortcut that offers superficial prestige, the author contends that current artists who delve into Shakespeare’s complex portrayals of human nature end up creating more nuanced and resonant works.\n\nWhich choice best states the main idea of the text?\n"", ""choices"": [""A) Shakespeare’s comedic subplots rarely influence modern comedic film or television, so they hold little relevance outside of stage-based performances."", ""B) Shakespeare’s approach to universal human themes has profoundly shaped modern storytelling across diverse media, maintaining enduring resonance."", ""C) Critics who question Shakespeare’s relevance today overlook the fact that his plays remain effective for superficial cultural references and literary allusions."", ""D) Among Shakespeare’s many contributions to theater, his focus on language is the only aspect that survives in contemporary artistic endeavors.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""The passage underscores Shakespeare’s continuing influence on the evolution of modern narrative structures, highlighting how his exploration of universal themes and his inventive dramatic techniques have shaped storytelling in a variety of creative forms. Choice B captures this overarching claim about Shakespeare’s far-reaching and enduring significance. Choice A is incorrect because the passage does not support the idea that Shakespeare’s comedic subplots are unimportant to modern media. Choice C misrepresents the text by implying that Shakespeare’s current value is primarily superficial. Choice D inaccurately restricts Shakespeare’s ongoing relevance to language alone, ignoring the other layers of his work discussed in the passage."", ""topic"": ""The significance of Shakespeare’s plays in modern storytelling""}",Main Idea,Hard,"The following text is adapted from a 21st-century commentary on the enduring impact of William Shakespeare’s works. The author discusses how Shakespeare’s plays continue to shape narrative structures in modern film, television, and stage productions, emphasizing that the Bard’s explorations of universal themes—such as regal ambition, unrequited devotion, and comedic misunderstandings—remain highly relevant to contemporary audiences. Additionally, the commentary posits that Shakespeare revolutionized dramatic storytelling by integrating rich layers of characterization, cunning wordplay, and metamorphic shifts in tone, setting a precedent for future generations of playwrights, screenwriters, and novelists. Although some critics argue that referencing Shakespeare has become merely a cultural shortcut that offers superficial prestige, the author contends that current artists who delve into Shakespeare’s complex portrayals of human nature end up creating more nuanced and resonant works.

Which choice best states the main idea of the text?
","A) Shakespeare’s comedic subplots rarely influence modern comedic film or television, so they hold little relevance outside of stage-based performances.
B) Shakespeare’s approach to universal human themes has profoundly shaped modern storytelling across diverse media, maintaining enduring resonance.
