2025-05-21 01:02:22,"{""skill"": ""Punctuation"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""Behavioral economics has demonstrated that a seemingly small choice, such as automatically enrolling employees in retirement plans, can have significant consequences for long-term savings. In a study investigating the influence of these “nudges” on financial decisions, many companies discovered that ________ despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing more consistently to their retirement accounts than they would have if required to opt in manually.\n\nWhich choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?"", ""choices"": [""A) that, despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing"", ""B) that; despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing"", ""C) that. Despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing"", ""D) that, despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment. Most employees ended up contributing""], ""answer"": ""A"", ""explanation"": ""Choice A is correct because the phrase “despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment” is treated as a parenthetical remark. Commas appropriately surround this additional information and keep the sentence structure clear. Choices B, C, and D create grammatical errors: using a semicolon (B) is incorrect because the phrase following “that” is not an independent clause; using a period (C) breaks the sentence into fragments; and separating the sentence after “enrollment” (D) results in an awkward split, making the second part a stand-alone clause without proper context."", ""topic"": ""Behavioral economics and its influence on financial decisions""}",Punctuation,Hard,"Behavioral economics has demonstrated that a seemingly small choice, such as automatically enrolling employees in retirement plans, can have significant consequences for long-term savings. In a study investigating the influence of these “nudges” on financial decisions, many companies discovered that ________ despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing more consistently to their retirement accounts than they would have if required to opt in manually.

