1. Redundancy of “that”:  
 • In your stem you write, “many companies discovered that ________ despite minimal awareness…” but every answer choice begins with “that…”. When the correct answer is inserted (choice A), the sentence reads “discovered that that, despite minimal awareness…” which is ungrammatical.  
 • To fix this, either remove “that” from the sentence stem before the blank or eliminate it from all answer choices. For example, if you remove “that” from the stem, the sentence would begin “many companies discovered ________ despite minimal awareness…” and then option A (“that, despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing”) plugs in correctly.

2. Difficulty Level Consideration:  
 • While the question effectively tests the comma placement around a parenthetical remark, it primarily examines one punctuation rule. Hard-level questions are expected to require students to apply multiple punctuation rules where different parts of the sentence interact.  
 • If you intend to keep this as a Hard question, consider integrating an additional punctuation element (such as a contrasting transition or a compound structure) so that students must juggle two or more rules. Otherwise, you might retitle the difficulty as Medium.

3. Formatting and Clarity:  
 • Your answer choices include only the tested phrase, which is good practice.  
 • The explanation is succinct and clearly explains why the other options are incorrect.

Taking these suggestions into account, here’s an example of how you might revise the stem:
 ""In a study investigating the influence of these 'nudges' on financial decisions, many companies discovered ________ despite minimal awareness of automatic enrollment, most employees ended up contributing more consistently to their retirement accounts than they would have if required to opt in manually.""
