ch is a coordinating conjunction.\n\nChoice D misuses a colon because the initial clause does not enumerate or introduce information in the way required for a colon. \n\nThus, accounting for the complexity of the punctuation in this sentence, a comma is the correct choice."", ""topic"": ""The revolutionary role of Che Guevara in Latin America"", ""total_score"": 22, ""constructive_feedback"": ""The sentence is clear and well-constructed. It correctly tests the student's ability to insert a comma after a parenthetical phrase before a coordinating conjunction. However, one issue is that the answer choices include both a period and a semicolon. Per our guidelines, a question should not offer both a semicolon and a period as distractors since they serve similar functions when joining independent clauses. Removing one of these options (or replacing it with a punctuation mark that does not conflict) would better align with the guidelines. Additionally, to truly meet a Hard difficulty level, you might consider adding an extra layer of complexity (e.g., involving simultaneous application of more than one punctuation rule) so that advanced students are adequately challenged."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""While the sentence involves a nonessential parenthetical element and checks for correct punctuation before a coordinating conjunction, the task is relatively straightforward. Hard questions should require the simultaneous application of multiple grammar rules. To better align the difficulty to a Hard level, you could incorporate an additional clause or punctuation nuance so that even students with strong skills must carefully consider the interplay between elements.""}"
