2025-05-21 01:20:27,"{""skill"": ""Referencing Data"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""Experts in finance and computing are investigating how quantum algorithms could streamline complex financial modeling scenarios. The table below shows hypothetical data on projected time savings and efficiency gains when using quantum computing, rather than classical high-performance computing (HPC), for several crucial financial tasks. Each of these tasks typically involves large-scale calculations, and researchers propose that quantum algorithms may significantly reduce run times. Based on these data, which choice best describes how quantum computing could deliver enhanced efficiency in certain financial modeling routines?\n\nProjected Efficiency Gains in Quantum Computing for Financial Modeling\n<table border=\""1\"">\n  <tr><th>Financial Task</th><th>Classical HPC Time (hours)</th><th>Quantum HPC Time (hours)</th><th>Projected Efficiency Gain (%)</th></tr>\n  <tr><td>Monte Carlo Simulations</td><td>3.2</td><td>1.8</td><td>43</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Portfolio Optimization</td><td>2.5</td><td>1.0</td><td>60</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Fair Value Calculation</td><td>5.0</td><td>2.3</td><td>54</td></tr>\n  <tr><td>Trade Execution</td><td>1.8</td><td>1.4</td><td>22</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nWhich choice best describes data from the table that support the claim that quantum computing can offer advanced efficiency in certain financial modeling tasks?"", ""choices"": [""A. Monte Carlo simulations show the highest projected efficiency gain at 43%."", ""B. Portfolio optimization has the greatest projected efficiency gain at 60%, indicating a substantially reduced run time from 2.5 hours to 1.0 hour."", ""C. Although the trade execution task demonstrates a 22% efficiency gain, it still requires more processing time with quantum methods than with classical HPC."", ""D. Fair value calculations see the smallest difference in run time of all tasks, highlighting an overall 2.7-hour improvement with quantum solutions.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""Choice B is correct because the table shows that portfolio optimization has the greatest efficiency gain (60%) of all the listed tasks. By reducing the run time from 2.5 hours for classical HPC to 1.0 hour for quantum solutions, it provides a substantially larger savings in both time and percentage than any other category. A is incorrect because 43% is not the highest gain. C is incorrect because trade execution still takes less time with quantum methods (1.4 hours) than with classical HPC (1.8 hours). D is incorrect because fair value calculation shows a larger time difference than trade execution, indicating it is not the smallest difference in run time."", ""topic"": ""The development of quantum computing in financial modeling""}",Referencing Data,Medium,"Experts in finance and computing are investigating how quantum algorithms could streamline complex financial modeling scenarios. The table below shows hypothetical data on projected time savings and efficiency gains when using quantum computing, rather than classical high-performance computing (HPC), for several crucial financial tasks. Each of these tasks typically involves large-scale calculations, and researchers propose that quantum algorithms may significantly reduce run times. Based on these data, which choice best describes how quantum computing could deliver enhanced efficiency in certain financial modeling routines?

