Choice A is incorrect because the People Power Revolution had the greatest number of participants in the table rather than the fewest. Choice C is incorrect because it reverses the durations of the two comparisons (the Burmese 8888 Uprising did not end faster than the People Power Revolution). Choice D is incorrect because the US Civil Rights Movement extended over many years, not just 3 months, and therefore does not reflect the table’s data accurately.",,,8,8,"The question is well-structured with a clear table title, labeled columns, and a final question that asks students to use data from the table—aligning well with SAT data interpretation standards. The answer choices are mostly plausible, and choice B (the correct answer) is supported by the table data. However, some distractors (especially D) are very obviously wrong, and the language in a couple of options could be polished (for example, clarifying numerical details) so that the differences require more careful reading. The explanation is insightful but could further emphasize why comparing, for instance, the Velvet Revolution and the Burmese 8888 Uprising best illustrates that fewer participants can lead to faster outcomes.",7,23,"{""skill"": ""Referencing Data"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""Selected Nonviolent Movements (1900\u0002000)\n\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\\textbf{Movement} & \\textbf{Key Year} & \\textbf{Approx. Participants} & \\textbf{Duration (Months)} \\\\\n\\hline\n\\text{Salt March (India)} & \\text{1930} & 80,000 & 1 \\\\\n\\text{People Power (Philippines)} & \\text{1986} & 2,000,000 & 3 \\\\\n\\text{Burmese 8888 Uprising (Myanmar)} & \\text{1988} & 1,000,000 & 6 \\\\\n\\text{Velvet Revolution (Czechoslovakia)} & \\text{1989} & 500,000 & 2 \\\\\n\\text{US Civil Rights Movement} & \\text{1963} & 250,000 & >150 \\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\nWhile larger crowds often draw increased attention, the duration and success of a movement can involve other factors, including differing political contexts and the reaction of those in power. From the table above, which example shows that smaller participant numbers do not necessarily equate to longer durations?\n"", ""choices"": [""A. The People Power Revolution had 2,000,000 participants and lasted 3 months compared to the longer Burmese 8888 Uprising's 6 months, which involved fewer participants."", ""B. The Velvet Revolution achieved its objectives in 2 months with 500,000 participants, whereas the Burmese 8888 Uprising took 6 months despite having 1,000,000 participants."", ""C. The US Civil Rights Movement, involving around 250,000 individuals in its key 1963 moment, was shorter than the Velvet Revolution."", ""D. The People Power Revolution and the US Civil Rights Movement show how movements with greater participants always focus on shorter decisive durations.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""Option B directly contrasts the Velvet Revolution (500,000 participants, 2 months duration) with the Burmese 8888 Uprising (1,000,000 participants, 6 months duration). It correctly demonstrates how a movement with fewer participants (the Velvet Revolution) achieved its goals more quickly than a movement with larger numbers. \n\nOption A incorrectly states that the People Power Revolution, with the largest number of participants (2,000,000), lasted 3 months, shorter than the Burmese 8888 Uprising at 6 months—this fails to highlight why fewer participants do not mean longer durations. Option C makes an inappropriate comparison, as the US Civil Rights Movement extended over decades, making the duration claim invalid. Option D is misinterpreted and factually wrong since higher participant counts do not definitively lead to shorter durations."", ""topic"": ""The role of nonviolent resistance in political movements"", ""total_score"": 23, ""constructive_feedback"": ""The question is well-structured with a clear table title, labeled columns, and a final question that asks students to use data from the table—aligning well with SAT data interpretation standards. The answer choices are mostly plausible, and choice B (the correct answer) is supported by the table data. However, some distractors (especially D) are very obviously wrong, and the language in a couple of options could be polished (for example, clarifying numerical details) so that the differences require more careful reading. The explanation is insightful but could further emphasize why comparing, for instance, the Velvet Revolution and the Burmese 8888 Uprising best illustrates that fewer participants can lead to faster outcomes."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""The labeled Medium difficulty is generally appropriate since the question requires moderate multi-step reasoning—namely, comparing two rows of data to draw a conclusion. To make the question even more aligned with the medium level, a slight increase in nuance (for example, including a secondary comparison element) could be considered so that there are less obvious eliminations among the choices.""}"
2025-05-21 01:24:08,"{""skill"": ""Referencing Data"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""Between 2005 and 2015, allegations of performance-enhancing drug use in sports remained a topic of heated debate. Regulatory agencies increased the number of tests administered at major international athletic competitions, yet many athletes continued to face suspensions or disqualifications. The table below presents information about doping tests, confirmed adverse results, and athlete disqualifications during four selected years.\n\nSummary of Doping Tests and Outcomes, 2005–2015\n<table border=\""1\"">\n<tr><th>Year</th><th>Doping Tests Conducted</th><th>Confirmed Adverse Results</th><th>Number of Disqualified Athletes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>2005</td><td>2,300</td><td>18</td><td>11</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2008</td><td>2,900</td><td>24</td><td>15</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2011</td><td>3,100</td><td>27</td><td>20</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2015</td><td>3,750</td><td>32</td><td>25</td></tr>\n</table>\n\nWhich choice is best supported by the data in the table regarding the continuing controversy over performance-enhancing drugs during these years?"", ""choices"": [""A. Between 2005 and 2008, doping tests more than tripled, but confirmed adverse results dropped from 18 to 15."", ""B. From 2008 to 2011, the number of tests decreased by over 1,000, yet disqualifications remained virtually identical."", ""C. In 2015, the largest number of tests was matched by the highest number of disqualifications, indicating doping remained a substantial concern."", ""D. From 2005 to 2015, the percentage of adverse results dropped substantially, which suggests doping activity declined over time.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""Choice C is correct because the data show an increase in the total number of doping tests (from 2,300 to 3,750) and a corresponding increase in disqualifications (from 11 to 25) by 2015. This pattern supports the idea that doping controversies remained significant since more testing coincided with more confirmed problems. Choices A, B, and D are not supported by the numerical changes in the table. For instance, the number of tests did not triple from 2005 to 2008 (making A incorrect), and tests did not decrease from 2008 to 2011 (refuting B). Finally, D misrepresents the data, which show that the number of adverse results rose in 2015 rather than declined, contradicting the claim that doping activity decreased."", ""topic"": ""The controversy surrounding performance-enhancing drugs in sports""}",Referencing Data,Medium,"Between 2005 and 2015, allegations of performance-enhancing drug use in sports remained a topic of heated debate. Regulatory agencies increased the number of tests administered at major international athletic competitions, yet many athletes continued to face suspensions or disqualifications. The table below presents information about doping tests, confirmed adverse results, and athlete disqualifications during four selected years.

