s, and location data to provide these benefits creates potential vulnerabilities that could lead to sensitive information being exposed. The analysts further highlight that while such tools significantly boost productivity, they often lack robust measures to fully safeguard user data.\n\nWhich finding from the analysts, if accurate, would best support the claim that AI assistants increase efficiency at the expense of personal privacy?"", ""choices"": [""A. Surveyed participants report rarely using AI assistants for unrelated tasks like public speaking preparation or playing games, largely sticking to organizational features."", ""B. Analytics show that advanced AI assistants can provide helpful suggestions without recording or analyzing user inputs, alleviating any privacy concerns."", ""C. A detailed study reveals that AI assistants are frequently utilized for their core productivity features, with users prioritizing efficiency over leisure applications."", ""D. Follow-up interviews reveal that while participants acknowledge improved task management, a significant number express concerns over the extensive personal data retained by their AI assistants.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""Choice D directly aligns with the claim that AI-powered personal assistants improve efficiency but raise privacy concerns. It illustrates that users benefit from the organizational features these tools offer but remain worried about the implications of the data collected. The other choices fail to show a clear link between efficiency and potential risks: A discusses usage behaviors unrelated to the claim, B undermines the data-privacy issue by suggesting it isn’t applicable, and C focuses exclusively on productivity, omitting privacy considerations."", ""topic"": ""The evolution of AI assistants and implications of their widespread use"", ""total_score"": 23, ""constructive_feedback"": ""The question is clearly written and aligns well with the SAT skill of identifying evidence that supports a claim. Answer choice D directly and correctly ties together the dual aspects of efficiency and privacy risks that the passage presents, and the distractors are mostly plausible enough to force careful consideration. However, some minor improvements would strengthen the item: expanding the passage slightly to meet the 90-word guideline for medium-difficulty questions would help increase the reading complexity and better match SAT standards, and revising details in distractors (e.g., referencing tasks not mentioned in the passage like 'currency conversion') would make all options more consistent with the source text."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""While the reasoning required is moderately multi-step and appropriate for medium difficulty, the passage is a bit shorter than recommended. Expanding the text by adding more context or additional details would require further analysis and better challenge both lower- and higher-level students. This would fully align the question with the medium-difficulty criteria.""}"
2025-05-21 01:38:58,"{""skill"": ""Supporting Claims"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""In Oscar Wilde’s The Picture of Dorian Gray, the morally questionable choices of the protagonist, Dorian, remain largely invisible in his outward appearance, which stays youthful and flawless. Instead, his portrait bears the burden of his corruption, reflecting each new moral transgression in its increasingly disturbing features. Wilde thus explores whether superficial beauty can conceal a corrupted nature and how consequences for one’s actions might be manifested elsewhere.\n\nWhich excerpt from the novel best supports the claim that Dorian’s external beauty conceals his moral failings by transferring all visible signs of decay to his portrait?"", ""choices"": [""A. A passage in which Basil accuses Dorian of irresponsibility but points out that the painting remains a minor detail of his life."", ""B. A passage in which Lord Henry praises Dorian’s refined manners but also wonders why Dorian is often so secretive."", ""C. A passage in which Dorian realizes the painting has become hideous while his own face is undiminished by sin."", ""D. A passage in which Dorian decides to move the portrait to another room without explaining his motives to Basil or Lord Henry.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""The best supporting evidence is the passage depicting how Dorian’s immoral behavior leaves no mark on his own outward image but instead distorts the painting, revealing his hidden corruption. Choice C clearly shows Dorian aware that the painting is now “hideous” while he remains unblemished. This detail directly connects Dorian’s declining morality to the visible ruin confined within the portrait, reinforcing the claim that his outer appearance does not reflect his wrongdoing. The other choices do not emphasize the painting’s transformation as compelling evidence of Dorian’s concealed moral decline."", ""topic"": ""The depiction of morality in The Picture of Dorian Gray by Oscar Wilde""}",Supporting Claims,Medium,"In Oscar Wilde’s The Picture of Dorian Gray, the morally questionable choices of the protagonist, Dorian, remain largely invisible in his outward appearance, which stays youthful and flawless. Instead, his portrait bears the burden of his corruption, reflecting each new moral transgression in its increasingly disturbing features. Wilde thus explores whether superficial beauty can conceal a corrupted nature and how consequences for one’s actions might be manifested elsewhere.

Which excerpt from the novel best supports the claim that Dorian’s external beauty conceals his moral failings by transferring all visible signs of decay to his portrait?","A. A passage in which Basil accuses Dorian of irresponsibility but points out that the painting remains a minor detail of his life.
B. A passage in which Lord Henry praises Dorian’s refined manners but also wonders why Dorian is often so secretive.
C. A passage in which Dorian realizes the painting has become hideous while his own face is undiminished by sin.
D. A passage in which Dorian decides to move the portrait to another room without explaining his motives to Basil or Lord Henry.",C,"The best supporting evidence is the passage depicting how Dorian’s immoral behavior leaves no mark on his own outward image but instead distorts the painting, revealing his hidden corruption. Choice C clearly shows Dorian aware that the painting is now “hideous” while he remains unblemished. This detail directly connects Dorian’s declining morality to the visible ruin confined within the portrait, reinforcing the claim that his outer appearance does not reflect his wrongdoing. The other choices do not emphasize the painting’s transformation as compelling evidence of Dorian’s concealed moral decline.",Previous input perfect; don't change anything.,"{""skill"": ""Supporting Claims"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""In Oscar Wilde\u0003\u0003\u0003s The Picture of Dorian Gray, the morally questionable choices of the protagonist, Dorian, remain largely invisible in his outward appearance, which stays youthful and flawless. Instead, his portrait bears the burden of his corruption, reflecting each new moral transgression in its increasingly disturbing features. Wilde thus explores whether superficial beauty can conceal a corrupted nature and how consequences for one\u0003\u0003s actions might be manifested elsewhere.\n\nWhich excerpt from the novel best supports the claim that Dorian\u0003\u0003s external beauty conceals his moral failings by transferring all visible signs of decay to his portrait?"", ""choices"": [""A. A passage in which Basil accuses Dorian of irresponsibility but points out that the painting remains a minor detail of his life."", ""B. A passage in which Lord Henry praises Dorian\u0003\u0003s refined manners but also wonders why Dorian is often so secretive."", ""C. A passage in which Dorian realizes the painting has become hideous while his own face is undiminished by sin."", ""D. A passage in which Dorian dec