directors who specialized in pantomime sequences were still in demand as technical advisers for silent-era revival events."", ""C) Vintage trade publications report that some significant production companies resisted implementing costly sound equipment, maintaining a steadfast commitment to costly set design."", ""D) Box office reviews indicate that between 1929 and 1931, films featuring dialogue outperformed silent releases by a wide margin, motivating studios to favor talkie productions.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""Choice D most strongly supports Davies’s claim because it shows that as talkies achieved greater box office success, studios would naturally invest in dialogue-centered storytelling, thereby eclipsing the silent-era focus on pantomime and visual spectacle. Heightened revenue from talkie productions would encourage the industry to prioritize sound technology and script development. Choices A and B suggest continued, though limited, interest in silent features, which does not confirm that talkies overshadowed them. Choice C shows reluctance to adopt new sound equipment, which would contradict Davies’s hypothesis of a broad shift taking place due to the rising appeal of talkies."", ""topic"": ""The transition from silent films to talkies in early cinema""}","Film historian Clara M. Davies contends that the emergence of voice-synchronized motion pictures catalyzed dramatic changes in American cinema, overshadowing many specifically visual storytelling conventions that had been ubiquitous during the silent era. She posits that with the arrival of talkies, studios diverted resources from the elaborate pantomime performances and decorative intertitles that previously defined theatrical spectacle, redirecting those resources toward developing sophisticated sound technology and dialogue-driven scripts. Davies further hypothesizes that this shift decisively recalibrated audience expectations, placing verbal articulation at the center of mainstream films.

