.\n\nIn the novel, Dickens frequently contrasts the opulence of the aristocracy with the poverty and desperation of the lower classes. For example, during the early stages of the revolution, the reader encounters scenes where once-oppressed peasants exact horrific vengeance on their former rulers, including acts that mirror the violence and neglect they themselves endured. Furthermore, Dickens's narrative explores how individuals such as Madame Defarge symbolize this cycle of retribution and escalate violence beyond mere justice to an unending cycle of cruelty.\n\nWhich hypothetical discovery, if true, would best support the claim that Dickens's portrayal of class struggle in *A Tale of Two Cities* emphasizes the cyclical nature of oppression?"", ""choices"": [""A) A draft of the novel contains notes suggesting that Dickens reduced scenes depicting the suffering of the aristocrats, focusing instead on their lapses in compassion."", ""B) Documents show that Dickens intended to heighten the revolutionary fervor by adding more references to public celebrations among the peasantry."", ""C) An early outline reveals that Dickens initially portrayed the peasants as more reluctant rebels who sought only a moderate change to social structures."", ""D) Newly found manuscript pages show peasants resorting to the same forms of cruelty that the nobility had previously inflicted on them.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""The claim that Dickens underscores a cyclical pattern of oppression hinges on textual evidence that the once oppressed adopt the same brutal tactics as their former oppressors. In this context, newly uncovered manuscript pages that demonstrate peasants enacting the same types of cruelty once inflicted upon them by the aristocracy would directly support the idea of an unbroken cycle of violence and oppression. The other choices focus on different narrative aspects or ideas, such as the depiction of aristocratic ineptitude or revolutionary joy, but do not directly reinforce the specific claim about cycles of oppression."", ""topic"": ""The themes of class struggle in A Tale of Two Cities by Charles Dickens"", ""total_score"": 24, ""constructive_feedback"": ""The question is well-constructed and aligns with SAT standards for testing the ability to identify evidence that supports a claim. It presents an engaging prompt that requires students to link the idea of cyclical oppression to a hypothetical manuscript discovery. The answer choices are plausible, with only one clearly matching the claim, and the explanation effectively clarifies why option D is best. To further improve the question, consider expanding the passage to include additional contextual details that underscore the cyclical nature of oppression. This extra detail would enhance the complexity appropriate for a hard-level question and provide even richer material for multi-step reasoning."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""While the question is labeled as Hard and employs complex vocabulary and multi-step reasoning, the reading passage is somewhat brief compared to typical hard-difficulty examples. Enhancing the passage with a bit more detail and layered context would better match its labeled difficulty and further challenge high-performing students.""}"
2025-05-21 01:51:28,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Easy"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• The Ottoman Empire emerged as a small state in Anatolia in the late 13th century.\n\n• The conquest of Constantinople in 1453 greatly expanded the empire’s reach.\n\n• Over the centuries, internal conflicts and weakening governance lessened its power.\n\n• The empire lost most of its territories during World War I.\n\n• It was officially dissolved in 1922.\n\nThe student wants to emphasize how the Ottoman Empire eventually collapsed in the early 20th century. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. The Ottoman Empire initially expanded from Anatolia but never extended beyond Constantinople."", ""B. Although once powerful, the Ottoman Empire lost most of its land during World War I and was officially dissolved by 1922."", ""C. In the late 13th century, the Ottoman Empire began as a small state in Anatolia before capturing Constantinople in 1453."", ""D. The Ottoman Empire steadily gained power through the 15th century, relying largely on its strategic location in Anatolia.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""Choice B is the best answer because it stresses the empire’s downfall, noting that it lost most territories during World War I and dissolved in 1922. Choice A mentions only expansion from Anatolia but not the eventual collapse. Choice C notes the empire’s early growth through capturing Constantinople but does not emphasize its end. Choice D focuses on how the empire gained power, leaving out its collapse."", ""topic"": ""The rise and fall of the Ottoman Empire""}",Synthesizing Notes,Easy,"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

