2025-05-21 01:54:14,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Easy"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Mitochondria are often called the \u001dpowerhouse\u001d of the cell because they help produce an abundant amount of ATP.\n\n• The Krebs cycle (also known as the citric acid cycle) takes place in the mitochondrial matrix.\n\n• The electron transport chain is embedded in the inner mitochondrial membrane.\n\n• Oxygen is required for most ATP production in mitochondria.\n\n\nThe student wants to highlight how mitochondria facilitate energy production for the cell. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. Mitochondria are present in most eukaryotic cells, but they do not function without oxygen."", ""B. Because mitochondria have their own DNA and can replicate, they can produce all the proteins needed for cellular energy."", ""C. The Krebs cycle in the matrix and the electron transport chain in the inner membrane work together with oxygen to generate ATP in mitochondria."", ""D. Oxygen passes freely into the cell, allowing the by-products of the Krebs cycle to leave the mitochondria for ATP production.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""Choice C is correct because it selects the relevant information: mitochondria rely on both the Krebs cycle in the matrix and the electron transport chain in the inner membrane, using oxygen to generate ATP. A is off topic because it only mentions oxygen but not how ATP is produced. B incorrectly suggests that mitochondria alone produce all necessary proteins for energy, which the notes do not state. D focuses on the concept of oxygen passing into the cell rather than how mitochondria facilitate energy production."", ""topic"": ""The role of mitochondria in cellular respiration""}",Synthesizing Notes,Easy,"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

• Mitochondria are often called the powerhouse of the cell because they help produce an abundant amount of ATP.

• The Krebs cycle (also known as the citric acid cycle) takes place in the mitochondrial matrix.

