2025-05-21 02:01:09,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Oedipus Rex by Sophocles centers on a prophecy that Oedipus will kill his father and marry his mother.\n\n• Determined to avoid that fate, Oedipus leaves Corinth.\n\n• He continuously tries to escape his destiny but ironically fulfills the prophecy by killing an unknown traveler who was his father.\n\n• Free will is illustrated through his decision to seek the truth about Laius’s murder.\n\n• Despite his attempts to avoid it, the prophecy ultimately comes true, highlighting destiny’s power.\n\nThe student wants to explain the tension between Oedipus’s sense of control and the prophecy’s inevitability to an audience unfamiliar with the play. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. In Oedipus Rex, Sophocles depicts a king who kills a stranger on his way to Thebes, not realizing that stranger is his father."", ""B. Despite Oedipus’s repeated decisions to escape the prophecy, he unwittingly fulfills it, thereby highlighting both his sense of free will and the inescapable power of fate."", ""C. In an effort to show Oedipus’s responsibility for Laius’s death, Sophocles emphasizes that Oedipus had been warned not to travel alone."", ""D. While Sophocles emphasizes the role of prophecy, the idea of free will emerges only when Oedipus meets Laius on the road to Thebes.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""Choice B incorporates Oedipus’s effort to flee the prophecy, his decision to investigate Laius’s murder, and the ultimate fulfillment of the prophecy. This highlights both his attempts at exercising free will and destiny’s unavoidable force. Choice A references Oedipus’s act of killing a stranger but doesn’t emphasize the tension between free will and fate. Choice C incorrectly claims Sophocles focuses chiefly on Oedipus’s responsibility, omitting the prophecy’s force. Choice D suggests free will appears only at the moment of Laius’s death, minimizing Oedipus’s ongoing attempts to control his destiny."", ""topic"": ""The exploration of destiny and free will in Oedipus Rex by Sophocles""}",Synthesizing Notes,Medium,"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

• Oedipus Rex by Sophocles centers on a prophecy that Oedipus will kill his father and marry his mother.

• Determined to avoid that fate, Oedipus leaves Corinth.

• He continuously tries to escape his destiny but ironically fulfills the prophecy by killing an unknown traveler who was his father.

• Free will is illustrated through his decision to seek the truth about Laius’s murder.

• Despite his attempts to avoid it, the prophecy ultimately comes true, highlighting destiny’s power.

The student wants to explain the tension between Oedipus’s sense of control and the prophecy’s inevitability to an audience unfamiliar with the play. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","A. In Oedipus Rex, Sophocles depicts a king who kills a stranger on his way to Thebes, not realizing that stranger is his father.
