•' rule as required, and the overall word count falls just below the minimum threshold for medium difficulty. Expanding the context slightly or adding one more bullet point could help ensure the question consistently meets medium-length expectations.",7,23,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Oedipus Rex by Sophocles centers on a prophecy that Oedipus will kill his father and marry his mother. \n\n• Determined to avoid that fate, Oedipus leaves Corinth, believing his parents reside there. \n\n• Despite trying to escape his destiny, Oedipus kills a stranger who he later discovers is his father. \n\n• Free will is illustrated through Oedipus’s decision to actively seek the truth about Laius’s murder, thus driving the plot forward. \n\n• The prophecy’s fulfillment underlines the inescapable power of destiny, even as human actions attempt to counter it. \n\n• Sophocles uses dramatic irony to enhance the audience's awareness of the prophecy Oedipus unwittingly fulfills. \n\nThe student wants to explain the tension between Oedipus’s sense of control and the prophecy’s inevitability to an audience unfamiliar with the play. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. In Oedipus Rex, Sophocles depicts a king who kills a stranger on his way to Thebes, not realizing that stranger is his father."", ""B. Despite Oedipus’s repeated decisions to escape the prophecy, he unwittingly fulfills it, thereby highlighting both his sense of free will and the inescapable power of fate."", ""C. In an effort to show Oedipus’s responsibility for Laius’s death, Sophocles emphasizes that Oedipus had been warned not to travel alone."", ""D. While Sophocles emphasizes the role of prophecy, the idea of free will emerges only when Oedipus meets Laius on the road to Thebes.""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""Choice B effectively synthesizes Oedipus's continuous attempts to evade his prophesied destiny and the eventual realization of its inevitability. It correctly highlights the duality of free will and fate, as presented in the notes. The other choices fail to address the critical tension between Oedipus’s perception of autonomy and the unyielding fulfillment of the prophecy."", ""topic"": ""The exploration of destiny and free will in Oedipus Rex by Sophocles"", ""total_score"": 23, ""constructive_feedback"": ""The question clearly targets synthesizing the tension between free will and destiny and the answer choices represent plausible alternatives with one clearly best option. However, the formatting of the bullet points does not strictly follow the '\n\n•' rule as required, and the overall word count falls just below the minimum threshold for medium difficulty. Expanding the context slightly or adding one more bullet point could help ensure the question consistently meets medium-length expectations."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""While the reasoning required is moderately complex and includes distractors that are tempting, the question is marginally short of the prescribed 100+ word count typical for medium questions. Increasing the word count and providing a bit more nuance in the notes would better align it with medium difficulty standards.""}"
2025-05-21 02:02:18,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic about the impact of the Agricultural Revolution on human societies, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• The transition from nomadic hunting and gathering to settled farming allowed for more reliable food sources.\n\n• Surplus grain gave rise to population growth and the formation of permanent settlements.\n\n• With more consistent access to food, people could specialize in tasks beyond food production, including pottery and metalwork.\n\n• Archaeological data show that early farming communities often developed new social hierarchies and trade networks.\n\n• Domestication of crops, such as wheat and barley, began in the Fertile Crescent approximately 10,000 years ago.\n\nThe student wants to explain how the surplus created by agriculture influenced the organization of human communities.\n\nWhich choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. After people began farming crops like wheat and barley, their food supplies became reliable enough to foster permanent settlements and job specialization."", ""B. The earliest permanent communities developed near rivers, where food sources and irrigation for crops were most abundant."", ""C. Domestication of crops in the Fertile Crescent helped communities produce the first known pottery and metal tools almost 10,000 years ago."", ""D. Nomadic hunter-gatherers often moved between diverse environments to ensure their survival, preventing permanent forms of social organization.""], ""answer"": ""A"", ""explanation"": ""Choice A best synthesizes the notes to explain how agricultural surpluses led to permanent settlements and specialized tasks. When food became more reliable, people could settle in one place, which encouraged society to become more organized and diverse in its roles. Choice B incorrectly focuses on geographical factors