 question is clear, logically structured, and effectively aligns with SAT standards for synthesizing notes. It presents well-organized bullet points and a clear objective that directs the student to synthesize information about agricultural surpluses and community organization. The answer choices are mostly plausible with one clearly correct choice, though the distractors could be slightly more nuanced. One area for improvement is to ensure the bullet points strictly adhere to the required formatting (i.e., using '\\n\\n•' with no extraneous whitespace) to maintain consistency."", ""difficulty_feedback"": ""The question meets the medium difficulty requirements by combining multi-step reasoning with moderately complex synthesis, and its length is within the acceptable range. To further enhance its challenge, consider revising one or two distractors to be even more tempting, perhaps by incorporating details that are partially correct but miss the full connection between agricultural surplus and social organization.""}"
2025-05-21 02:03:50,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Legal scholar Kimberlé Crenshaw introduced the term intersectionality in 1989 to explain overlapping forms of discrimination.\n\n• People can simultaneously identify with multiple social categories — including race, gender, sexuality, and class — that magnify or worsen prejudice.\n\n• Intersectionality highlights the ways racism, sexism, homophobia, and other biases interact.\n\n• Those with multiple marginalized identities often face additional hurdles in healthcare, employment, and education.\n\nThe student wants to clarify a basic definition of intersectionality for an audience that is aware discrimination exists but has not considered how overlapping social identities intensify prejudice.\n\nWhich choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. Intersectionality is a term that broadly covers racism, sexism, and other biases that occur separately in different groups."", ""B. Although many people hold multiple social identities, discrimination is often direct and stems from a single defining characteristic."", ""C. Prejudice increases when individuals experience only one form of bias, such as racism, according to the theory of intersectionality."", ""D. Intersectionality, introduced by Kimberlé Crenshaw in 1989, explains that people with more than one marginalized identity can encounter overlapping discrimination.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""Choice D is correct because it clearly defines intersectionality as introduced by Crenshaw and explains that overlapping social identities can aggravate discrimination. Choice A is incorrect because it suggests each form of bias is separate, disregarding how intersectionality addresses overlapping issues. Choice B is incorrect for implying that discrimination typically stems from just one defining characteristic. Choice C is incorrect because it misrepresents the concept, claiming it addresses only a single form of bias, whereas the notes make clear that intersectionality involves multiple, interacting forms of oppression."", ""topic"": ""How intersectionality shapes experiences of discrimination""}",Synthesizing Notes,Medium,"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

