2025-05-21 02:08:01,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• Epistemic injustice is a concept referring to unfair practices that systematically discredit knowledge from individuals in marginalized communities.\n\n• Testimonial injustice occurs when testimonies from marginalized individuals are disbelieved or undervalued based on bias.\n\n• Hermeneutical injustice takes place when important social or cultural experiences of marginalized groups are not well understood or captured in mainstream discourse.\n\n• Researcher Kelsey Yu studied teachers in under-resourced schools who frequently dismissed students’ accounts of local environmental hazards.\n\n\nThe student wants to describe for an unfamiliar audience how disregarding first-hand accounts can perpetuate epistemic injustice in marginalized communities. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. Epistemic injustice occasionally causes the dismissal of scientific studies, including those from well-funded institutions that neglect marginalized groups."", ""B. Disbelieving certain testimonies can prove harmful, but hermeneutical injustice is more serious because it outweighs other forms of bias among educators."", ""C. Teachers in under-resourced schools can perpetuate epistemic injustice by dismissing local students’ testimonies about environmental hazards, illustrating how marginalized voices are systematically undervalued."", ""D. Epistemic injustice has arisen as a field of study focused on bridging cultural divides and clarifying how miscommunication occurs among diverse populations.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""Choice C best accomplishes the student’s goal by directly explaining how disregarding students’ testimonials about local environmental hazards illustrates a form of epistemic injustice. It combines information about teachers in under-resourced schools with the concept of systematically undervalued testimony.\nChoice A is incorrect because it emphasizes scientific studies rather than marginalized testimonies. Choice B incorrectly suggests that hermeneutical injustice is definitively more serious than testimonial injustice and does not address the teacher-student dynamic. Choice D is too broad, discussing the field as a whole rather than focusing on how dismissing firsthand accounts perpetuates epistemic injustice in marginalized communities."", ""topic"": ""The role of epistemic injustice in marginalized communities""}",Synthesizing Notes,Medium,"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

• Epistemic injustice is a concept referring to unfair practices that systematically discredit knowledge from individuals in marginalized communities.

