zards, illustrating how marginalized voices are systematically undervalued."", ""D. Epistemic injustice has arisen as a field of study focused on bridging cultural divides and clarifying how miscommunication occurs among diverse populations.""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""Choice C best accomplishes the student\u0001s goal by directly explaining how disregarding students\u0001 testimonials about local environmental hazards illustrates a form of epistemic injustice. It combines information about teachers in under-resourced schools with the concept of systematically undervalued testimony.\nChoice A is incorrect because it emphasizes scientific studies rather than marginalized testimonies. Choice B incorrectly suggests that hermeneutical injustice is definitively more serious than testimonial injustice and does not address the teacher-student dynamic. Choice D is too broad, discussing the field as a whole rather than focusing on how dismissing firsthand accounts perpetuates epistemic injustice in marginalized communities."", ""topic"": ""The role of epistemic injustice in marginalized communities""}","While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:

