2025-05-21 02:15:56,"{""skill"": ""Synthesizing Notes"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:\n\n• The Tiananmen Square protests of 1989 were initially sparked in April by university students advocating political transparency and broader economic liberalization.\n\n• The government’s military crackdown on June 4 resulted in a significant number of casualties and widespread international condemnation.\n\n• Many foreign governments imposed sanctions, straining China’s diplomatic and trade relations.\n\n• Domestic media outlets were forced to suppress coverage of the incident, reinforcing a decades-long pattern of state censorship.\n\n• Despite the political repression, China continued to pursue economic reforms, leading to accelerated market growth in the 1990s.\n\n\nThe student wants to illustrate how international censure intersected with China’s policy decisions, emphasizing how the protests shaped both global perceptions and internal reforms.\n\nWhich choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?"", ""choices"": [""A. Although China’s government remained focused on economic liberalization, foreign governments did not respond with any major sanctions or public reactions."", ""B. By June 4, 1989, thousands of university students embraced the idea of peaceful public gatherings, choosing to highlight state censorship and global responses simultaneously."", ""C. Despite the protests occurring in April, subsequent military crackdowns and unchanged foreign policies illustrated how domestic events rarely affect international perceptions of a nation’s governance."", ""D. International condemnation and sanctions followed the June 4 crackdown, yet China maintained strict state censorship as it continued economic reforms, shaping its relationships abroad and its policies at home.""], ""answer"": ""D"", ""explanation"": ""Choice D synthesizes multiple notes: it 