However, because the intended difficulty is Hard, the question would be stronger if it required students to infer multiple grammar rules—not just choosing the proper tense but also navigating a complex subject-verb agreement scenario. Hard questions on the SAT should combine nuances (for example, a tricky subject within a long noun phrase) to test advanced grammar skills.

To increase the difficulty to Hard, consider revising the sentence to force students to identify the true subject when additional phrases intervene. For example, you might change the sentence to:

