With these small revisions, the question will fully comply with all the SAT style and formatting guidelines.","{""skill"": ""Geometry"", ""difficulty"": ""Medium"", ""question"": ""Quadrilateral PQRS is inscribed in a circle, so angles $\\angle P$ and $\\angle R$ are opposite angles, and angles $\\angle Q$ and $\\angle S$ are opposite angles. The measure of $\\angle P$ is $2x + 10$, the measure of $\\angle Q$ is $3x - 20$, and the measure of $\\angle R$ is $70$.\n\nWhat is the measure, in degrees, of $\\angle S$?"", ""choices"": [""A) 30"", ""B) 50"", ""C) 70"", ""D) 110""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""First, note that in a quadrilateral inscribed in a circle, opposite angles sum to $180$ degrees.\n\nStep 1: Use $\\angle P + \\angle R = 180$. That is, \\[(2x + 10) + 70 = 180.\\]\n\nStep 2: Simplify to find $x$:\n\\[2x + 80 = 180\\]\n\\[2x = 100\\]\n\\[x = 50.\\]\n\nStep 3: Substitute $x = 50$ back into $2x + 10$ to confirm $\\angle P$:\n\\[2(50) + 10 = 110.\\]\n\nStep 4: Next, use $\\angle Q + \\angle S = 180$. Since \\[\\angle Q = 3(50) - 20 = 150 - 20 = 130,\\] $\\angle S$ is \\[180 - 130 = 50.\\]\n\nThus, the measure of $\\angle S$ is $50$ degrees."", ""topic"": ""Inscribed Quadrilaterals""}","Quadrilateral PQRS is inscribed in a circle, so angles $\angle P$ and $\angle R$ are opposite angles, and angles $\angle Q$ and $\angle S$ are opposite angles. The measure of $\angle P$ is $2x + 10$, the measure of $\angle Q$ is $3x - 20$, and the measure of $\angle R$ is $70$.

