2025-05-21 11:22:12,"{""skill"": ""Geometry"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""In trapezoid PQRS with PQ parallel to RS, the diagonals PR and QS intersect at T. It is given that $PT : TR = 3 : 2$ and that PQ is 20 units longer than RS.\n\nIf the area of trapezoid PQRS is 800 square units, what is the length, in units, of PQ?"", ""choices"": [""A. 60"", ""B. 50"", ""C. 55"", ""D. 66""], ""answer"": ""A"", ""explanation"": ""First, recognize that in a trapezoid, the intersection of its diagonals divides them into segments in the same ratio as the lengths of the parallel sides. Since $PT : TR = 3 : 2$, this implies that $PQ : RS = 3 : 2.$ Let $RS = x.$ Because $PQ$ is 20 units longer than $RS,$ we have $PQ = x + 20.$ Setting up the ratio $\\frac{x + 20}{x} = \\frac{3}{2}$ leads to $2(x + 20) = 3x,$ or $2x + 40 = 3x,$ giving $x = 40.$ Therefore, $RS = 40$ and $PQ = 60.$\n\nNext, recall that the area of a trapezoid is $\\frac{1}{2} (\\text{sum of parallel sides}) \\times (\\text{height}).$ If $PQ + RS = 60 + 40 = 100,$ then $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 100 \\cdot (\\text{height}) = 800.$ Solving yields $50 \\times (\\text{height}) = 800,$ which means the height is 16. Since all the given conditions are satisfied with $PQ = 60,$ that must be the length of the longer base, PQ."", ""topic"": ""Trapezoids and diagonal ratios""}",Geometry,Hard,"In trapezoid PQRS with PQ parallel to RS, the diagonals PR and QS intersect at T. It is given that $PT : TR = 3 : 2$ and that PQ is 20 units longer than RS.

If the area of trapezoid PQRS is 800 square units, what is the length, in units, of PQ?","A. 60
B. 50
