","{""skill"": ""Linear Equations"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""Line $m$ is defined by $3x + 2y = 12$. Line $n$ is defined by $x - 4y = 8$.\n\nThese two lines intersect at point $T$. A third line $p$ passes through $T$ and has slope $-\\frac{1}{5}$. Which of the following is the $y$-intercept of line $p$?"", ""choices"": [""A) -\\frac{1}{35}"", ""B) \\frac{2}{35}"", ""C) \\frac{3}{35}"", ""D) \\frac{1}{7}""], ""answer"": ""B"", ""explanation"": ""First, find the coordinates of point $T$ by solving the system of equations. From $x - 4y = 8$, express $x$ in terms of $y$ and substitute into $3x + 2y = 12$. Solving gives $T = \\left(\\frac{32}{7}, -\\frac{6}{7}\\right)$. Next, use the point-slope form for line $p$ with slope $-\\frac{1}{5}$ through $T$. The equation becomes \\(y - \\left(-\\frac{6}{7}\\right) = -\\frac{1}{5}(x - \\frac{32}{7})\\). Simplifying yields \\(y = -\\frac{1}{5}x + \\frac{2}{35}\\), so the $y$-intercept of line $p$ is \\(\\frac{2}{35}\\). Choices A, C, and D are incorrect because they result from common errors, such as arithmetic mistakes while solving the system or misstepping in simplifying the point-slope equation. For example, choice A might result from a negation error in one of the terms, choice C might involve a fraction simplification mistake, and choice D might mix up the coordinates or the slope."", ""topic"": ""Intersecting lines and point-slope form""}","Line $m$ is defined by $3x + 2y = 12$. Line $n$ is defined by $x - 4y = 8$.

