2025-05-21 13:18:03,"{""skill"": ""Linear Word Problems"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""An orchard manager has enough space to plant exactly 96 fruit trees. The orchard manager can choose only apple trees and peach trees. Each apple tree costs $30$ to plant, and each peach tree costs $50$ to plant. The orchard manager has a total budget of $3{,}600$ dollars.\n\nWhat is the maximum number of peach trees that can be planted without exceeding the budget, if all 96 spaces must be completely filled?"", ""choices"": [""A. 32"", ""B. 34"", ""C. 36"", ""D. 38""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""First, let $A$ be the number of apple trees and $P$ be the number of peach trees. Since all 96 spots must be filled, $A + P = 96$. The total planting cost must not exceed $3{,}600$, which translates to the inequality $30A + 50P \\le 3{,}600$.\n\nSubstitute $A = 96 - P$ into the cost inequality:\\\n\\(30(96 - P) + 50P \\le 3{,}600\\).\\\nSimplify:\\\n\\(2{,}880 - 30P + 50P \\le 3{,}600\\)\\\n\\(2{,}880 + 20P \\le 3{,}600\\)\\\n\\(20P \\le 720\\)\\\n\\(P \\le 36\\).\\\n\nSo the maximum possible value of $P$ is 36. Therefore, the orchard manager can plant 36 peach trees while still meeting the required constraints."", ""topic"": ""Orchard budget and space constraints""}",Linear Word Problems,Hard,"An orchard manager has enough space to plant exactly 96 fruit trees. The orchard manager can choose only apple trees and peach trees. Each apple tree costs $30$ to plant, and each peach tree costs $50$ to plant. The orchard manager has a total budget of $3{,}600$ dollars.

What is the maximum number of peach trees that can be planted without exceeding the budget, if all 96 spaces must be completely filled?","A. 32
