2025-05-21 14:20:45,"{""skill"": ""Quadratic Equations"", ""difficulty"": ""Hard"", ""question"": ""An object is launched upward from a 50-foot platform. The height in feet of the object at time t seconds is given by the function $h(t) = -16t^2 + vt + 50$, where v is a positive constant.\n\nThe object touches the ground exactly once at some time T > 0, meaning $h(T) = 0$. If the object also reaches a maximum height of 130 feet, which of the following values of v is closest to the actual value?"", ""choices"": [""A) 70.0"", ""B) 71.0"", ""C) 71.6"", ""D) 72.0""], ""answer"": ""C"", ""explanation"": ""First, the time T when the object hits the ground satisfies $-16T^2 + vT + 50 = 0$. Next, the object reaches its maximum height at $t = \\frac{v}{2(16)} = \\frac{v}{32}$. Substituting $t = \\frac{v}{32}$ into the function $h(t)$, we get $130 = -16\\left(\\frac{v}{32}\\right)^2 + v\\left(\\frac{v}{32}\\right) + 50$. Simplifying yields $130 = -\\frac{v^2}{64} + \\frac{v^2}{32} + 50$, which further simplifies to $130 = \\frac{v^2}{64} + 50$. Therefore, \\(\\frac{v^2}{64} = 80\\), so \\(v^2 = 5120\\), giving \\(v = \\sqrt{5120}\\), which is approximately 71.6. Hence choice C is correct. One may check that this value of v also gives a positive time T when \\(h(T) = 0\\)."", ""topic"": ""Quadratic equations with real-world context""}",Quadratic Equations,Hard,"An object is launched upward from a 50-foot platform. The height in feet of the object at time t seconds is given by the function $h(t) = -16t^2 + vt + 50$, where v is a positive constant.

